{"timeLimit":3600,"questions":[{"question":"Express \\( 2021 \\) in Roman numerals","options":["MXI","MXX","MMXII","MMXXI","MMXII"],"correctIndex":3,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\( 2021 = 2000 + 20 + 1 \\)","\\( 2000 = \\text{MM} \\)","\\( 20 = \\text{XX} \\)","\\( 1 = \\text{I} \\)","\\( \\therefore 2021 = \\text{MMXXI} \\)"],"hint":"Break the number into thousands, hundreds, tens, and units: \\( \\text{M} = 1000 \\), \\( \\text{X} = 10 \\), \\( \\text{I} = 1 \\).","image":null,"topic":null,"type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"Express four thousand and sixty two in figures","options":["\\( 40062 \\)","\\( 4062 \\)","\\( 460 \\)","\\( 4602 \\)","\\( 400062 \\)"],"correctIndex":1,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\( \\text{Four thousand} = 4000 \\)","\\( \\text{Sixty-two} = 62 \\)","\\( 4000 + 62 = 4062 \\)"],"hint":"Identify the value of each digit based on its place value (thousands, hundreds, tens, units).","image":null,"topic":null,"type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"Write in words: \\( 10079 \\)","options":["One hundred and seventy nine","Ten thousand and seventy nine","Ten thousand seven hundred and nine","One hundred and seven thousand and nine","One thousand seven hundred and nine"],"correctIndex":1,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\( 10079 = 10{,}079 \\)","\\( 10 \\) is in the thousands period \\( \\Rightarrow \\text{Ten thousand} \\)","\\( 079 \\) is in the ones period \\( \\Rightarrow \\text{seventy-nine} \\)","\\( \\therefore 10079 = \\text{Ten thousand and seventy nine} \\)"],"hint":"Group the digits into periods (thousands, ones) to read the number correctly.","image":null,"topic":null,"type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"The average of \\( 8, 8, 9, 11, 22 \\) and \\( 26 \\) is?","options":["\\( 13 \\)","\\( 14 \\)","\\( 15 \\)","\\( 16 \\)","\\( 17 \\)"],"correctIndex":1,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\( \\text{Sum} = 8 + 8 + 9 + 11 + 22 + 26 \\)","\\( = 84 \\)","\\( n = 6 \\)","\\( \\text{Average} = \\dfrac{84}{6} \\)","\\( = 14 \\)"],"hint":"Add all the numbers together and divide by the total number of items.","image":null,"topic":null,"type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"Find the area of a circle of radius \\( 10 \\text{ cm} \\). (Take \\( \\pi = 3.14 \\))","options":["\\( 10 \\text{ cm}^2 \\)","\\( 100 \\text{ cm}^2 \\)","\\( 30 \\text{ cm}^2 \\)","\\( 31.4 \\text{ cm}^2 \\)","\\( 314 \\text{ cm}^2 \\)"],"correctIndex":4,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\( A = \\pi r^2 \\)","\\( = 3.14 \\times (10)^2 \\)","\\( = 3.14 \\times 100 \\)","\\( = 314 \\text{ cm}^2 \\)"],"hint":"Use the formula for the area of a circle: \\( A = \\pi r^2 \\).","image":null,"topic":null,"type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"How many faces does a triangular prism have?","options":["Four","Five","Six","Eight","Three"],"correctIndex":1,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\( \\text{Triangular bases} = 2 \\)","\\( \\text{Rectangular faces} = 3 \\)","\\( \\text{Total faces} = 2 + 3 = 5 \\)"],"hint":"A triangular prism has two triangular bases and rectangular sides connecting corresponding sides of the triangles.","image":"/exam-archive/competitions/scholastic/upper-primary/2025/first-Round/q6.svg","topic":null,"type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"What type of angle is \\( 98^{\\circ} \\)?","options":["Acute angle","Obtuse angle","Right angle","Reflex angle","Angle at a point"],"correctIndex":1,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\( \\text{Acute angle: } 0^{\\circ} < \\theta < 90^{\\circ} \\)","\\( \\text{Right angle: } \\theta = 90^{\\circ} \\)","\\( \\text{Obtuse angle: } 90^{\\circ} < \\theta < 180^{\\circ} \\)","\\( \\text{Reflex angle: } 180^{\\circ} < \\theta < 360^{\\circ} \\)","\\( 90^{\\circ} < 98^{\\circ} < 180^{\\circ} \\)","\\( \\therefore 98^{\\circ} \\text{ is an Obtuse angle} \\)"],"hint":"Recall the definitions of different types of angles based on their measure in degrees.","image":null,"topic":null,"type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"The triangle has a base of \\( 5 \\text{ m} \\) and a height of \\( 6 \\text{ m} \\). What is the area of the triangle in \\( \\text{m}^2 \\)?","options":["\\( 60 \\)","\\( 30 \\)","\\( 17 \\)","\\( 15 \\)","\\( 8.5 \\)"],"correctIndex":3,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\( A = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\times b \\times h \\)","\\( = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\times 5 \\times 6 \\)","\\( = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\times 30 \\)","\\( = 15 \\text{ m}^2 \\)"],"hint":"Use the formula: \\( A = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\times b \\times h \\).","image":"/exam-archive/competitions/scholastic/upper-primary/2025/first-Round/q8.svg","topic":null,"type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"How many halves \\( \\left( \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right) \\) are contained in \\( 2 \\) wholes?","options":["\\( 1 \\)","\\( 2 \\)","\\( 3 \\)","\\( 4 \\)","\\( 5 \\)"],"correctIndex":3,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\( 2 \\div \\dfrac{1}{2} \\)","\\( = 2 \\times 2 \\)","\\( = 4 \\)"],"hint":"Divide \\( 2 \\) by \\( \\dfrac{1}{2} \\) using the reciprocal.","image":null,"topic":null,"type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"In the triangle below, what is the value of the angle marked \\( x^{\\circ} \\)?","options":["\\( 30^{\\circ} \\)","\\( 50^{\\circ} \\)","\\( 60^{\\circ} \\)","\\( 90^{\\circ} \\)","\\( 120^{\\circ} \\)"],"correctIndex":2,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\( x + 90^{\\circ} + 30^{\\circ} = 180^{\\circ} \\)","\\( x + 120^{\\circ} = 180^{\\circ} \\)","\\( x = 180^{\\circ} - 120^{\\circ} \\)","\\( x = 60^{\\circ} \\)"],"hint":"The sum of angles in a triangle is \\( 180^{\\circ} \\). One angle is \\( 90^{\\circ} \\) (right angle) and another is \\( 30^{\\circ} \\).","image":"/exam-archive/competitions/scholastic/upper-primary/2025/first-Round/q10.svg","topic":null,"type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"Calculate the LCM of \\( 2, 8 \\) and \\( 12 \\).","options":["\\( 2 \\)","\\( 24 \\)","\\( 36 \\)","\\( 72 \\)","\\( 96 \\)"],"correctIndex":1,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\( 2 = 2^1 \\)","\\( 8 = 2^3 \\)","\\( 12 = 2^2 \\times 3 \\)","\\( \\text{LCM} = 2^3 \\times 3 \\)","\\( = 8 \\times 3 \\)","\\( = 24 \\)"],"hint":"List the multiples of each number or use prime factorization to find the least common multiple.","image":null,"topic":null,"type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"The HCF of \\( 14 \\) and \\( 28 \\)","options":["\\( 2 \\)","\\( 7 \\)","\\( 56 \\)","\\( 28 \\)","\\( 14 \\)"],"correctIndex":4,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\( \\text{Factors of } 14 = \\{1, 2, 7, 14\\} \\)","\\( \\text{Factors of } 28 = \\{1, 2, 4, 7, 14, 28\\} \\)","\\( \\text{Common factors} = \\{1, 2, 7, 14\\} \\)","\\( \\text{HCF} = 14 \\)"],"hint":"Find the factors of each number and identify the highest factor common to both.","image":null,"topic":null,"type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"What is the value of \\( 2.75 + 0.003 + 0.158 \\)?","options":["\\( 4.36 \\)","\\( 2.911 \\)","\\( 0.436 \\)","\\( 2.938 \\)","\\( 3.5 \\)"],"correctIndex":1,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\( \\phantom{+0.}2.750 \\)","\\( + 0.003 \\)","\\( + 0.158 \\)","\\( = 2.911 \\)"],"hint":"Align the decimal points vertically before adding the numbers.","image":null,"topic":null,"type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"\\( 25 \\) added to a number makes it half a gross. What is the number?","options":["\\( 35 \\)","\\( 72 \\)","\\( 97 \\)","\\( 47 \\)","\\( 100 \\)"],"correctIndex":3,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\( 1 \\text{ gross} = 144 \\)","\\( \\text{Half a gross} = \\dfrac{144}{2} = 72 \\)","\\( x + 25 = 72 \\)","\\( x = 72 - 25 \\)","\\( x = 47 \\)"],"hint":"A gross \\( = 144 \\). Find half a gross, then set up \\( x + 25 = \\text{half gross} \\) and solve.","image":null,"topic":null,"type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"A box weighs \\( 2.25 \\text{ kg} \\). What will \\( 20 \\) boxes weigh?","options":["\\( 12.7 \\text{ kg} \\)","\\( 25.4 \\text{ kg} \\)","\\( 22.5 \\text{ kg} \\)","\\( 45.0 \\text{ kg} \\)","\\( 22.25 \\text{ kg} \\)"],"correctIndex":3,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\( \\text{Total weight} = 2.25 \\times 20 \\)","\\( 2.25 \\times 10 = 22.5 \\)","\\( 22.5 \\times 2 = 45.0 \\)","\\( = 45.0 \\text{ kg} \\)"],"hint":"Multiply the weight of one box by the total number of boxes.","image":null,"topic":null,"type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"A 3-D shape has been opened up to a flat 2-D shape (a net) and is shown below. What was the 3-D shape?","options":["Sphere","Cuboid","Cone","Cube","Cylinder"],"correctIndex":4,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\( \\text{Net} = 1 \\text{ rectangle} + 2 \\text{ circles} \\)","\\( \\text{Rectangle} \\Rightarrow \\text{curved surface (tube)} \\)","\\( \\text{2 circles} \\Rightarrow \\text{top and bottom bases} \\)","\\( \\therefore \\text{3-D shape} = \\text{Cylinder} \\)"],"hint":"The net consists of one large rectangle and two identical circles — these form the body and bases of the shape.","image":"/exam-archive/competitions/scholastic/upper-primary/2025/first-Round/q16.svg","topic":null,"type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"If there are \\( 9 \\) squares on each face of a Rubik's Cube, how many squares are there in total?","options":["\\( 15 \\)","\\( 27 \\)","\\( 45 \\)","\\( 54 \\)","\\( 60 \\)"],"correctIndex":3,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\( \\text{Faces on a cube} = 6 \\)","\\( \\text{Squares per face} = 9 \\)","\\( \\text{Total squares} = 6 \\times 9 \\)","\\( = 54 \\)"],"hint":"Determine the total number of faces on a cube, then multiply by the number of squares per face.","image":"/exam-archive/competitions/scholastic/upper-primary/2025/first-Round/q17.svg","topic":null,"type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"The ________ of a circle is the distance from the center of a circle to any point on the circumference","options":["Radius","Diameter","Center","Chord","Circumference"],"correctIndex":0,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\( \\text{Radius} = \\text{center} \\to \\text{any point on circumference} \\)","\\( \\text{Diameter} = 2 \\times \\text{radius} \\)","\\( \\therefore \\text{The answer is Radius.} \\)"],"hint":"Recall the basic terminology of a circle.","image":null,"topic":null,"type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"What fact makes a cube unique and makes its surface area easy to determine?","options":["It's a three-dimensional shape","It is the only three-dimensional shape whose area is expressed in square units.","It always consists of six edges.","Its surface always consists of six squares that are equal in size and area.","None of the above"],"correctIndex":3,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\( \\text{Cube: all faces are identical squares} \\)","\\( \\text{Number of faces} = 6 \\)","\\( \\text{SA} = 6 \\times s^2 \\)","\\( \\therefore \\text{All 6 faces are equal squares — this is unique to a cube.} \\)"],"hint":"Think about the specific properties of the faces of a cube compared to other 3D shapes like a cuboid.","image":null,"topic":null,"type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"The value of \\( 11^2 \\) is?","options":["\\( 16 \\)","\\( 22 \\)","\\( 64 \\)","\\( 81 \\)","\\( 121 \\)"],"correctIndex":4,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\( 11^2 = 11 \\times 11 \\)","\\( = 121 \\)"],"hint":"Squaring a number means multiplying it by itself.","image":null,"topic":null,"type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"One quintillion is?","options":["\\( 1 \\times 10^9 \\)","\\( 1 \\times 10^{12} \\)","\\( 1 \\times 10^{15} \\)","\\( 1 \\times 10^{18} \\)","\\( 1 \\times 10^{100} \\)"],"correctIndex":3,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\( \\text{Million} = 1 \\times 10^6 \\)","\\( \\text{Billion} = 1 \\times 10^9 \\)","\\( \\text{Trillion} = 1 \\times 10^{12} \\)","\\( \\text{Quadrillion} = 1 \\times 10^{15} \\)","\\( \\text{Quintillion} = 1 \\times 10^{18} \\)"],"hint":"Recall the sequence: million \\( (10^6) \\), billion \\( (10^9) \\), trillion \\( (10^{12}) \\), quadrillion \\( (10^{15}) \\).","image":null,"topic":null,"type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"Which numeral is in the tenths place of the number \\( 53.04 \\)?","options":["\\( 5 \\)","\\( 4 \\)","\\( 3 \\)","\\( 0 \\)","None of the above"],"correctIndex":3,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\( 53.04 \\)","\\( 5 \\Rightarrow \\text{tens place} \\)","\\( 3 \\Rightarrow \\text{ones place} \\)","\\( 0 \\Rightarrow \\text{tenths place} = 10^{-1} \\)","\\( 4 \\Rightarrow \\text{hundredths place} = 10^{-2} \\)","\\( \\therefore \\text{tenths digit} = 0 \\)"],"hint":"The first digit after the decimal point is the tenths place, the second is hundredths.","image":null,"topic":null,"type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"George left home at \\( 7{:}30 \\text{ a.m.} \\) for school. School begins at \\( 8{:}45 \\text{ a.m.} \\) George was \\( 10 \\) minutes late. How long did George take to get to school?","options":["\\( 1 \\text{ hr } 25 \\text{ mins} \\)","\\( 1 \\text{ hr } 55 \\text{ mins} \\)","\\( 3 \\text{ hrs } 25 \\text{ mins} \\)","\\( 4 \\text{ hrs } 55 \\text{ mins} \\)","\\( 30 \\text{ mins} \\)"],"correctIndex":0,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\( \\text{Arrival time} = 8{:}45 + 10 \\text{ min} = 8{:}55 \\text{ a.m.} \\)","\\( \\text{Departure time} = 7{:}30 \\text{ a.m.} \\)","\\( 7{:}30 \\to 8{:}30 = 1 \\text{ hour} \\)","\\( 8{:}30 \\to 8{:}55 = 25 \\text{ minutes} \\)","\\( \\text{Total} = 1 \\text{ hr } 25 \\text{ mins} \\)"],"hint":"George arrived \\( 10 \\) minutes after \\( 8{:}45 \\). Find the difference between arrival time and departure time.","image":null,"topic":null,"type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"At what speed does a man walk to cover \\( 12 \\text{ km} \\) in \\( 3 \\text{ hours} \\)?","options":["\\( 36 \\text{ kph} \\)","\\( 4 \\text{ kph} \\)","\\( 6 \\text{ kph} \\)","\\( 3 \\text{ kph} \\)","\\( 2 \\text{ kph} \\)"],"correctIndex":1,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\( \\text{Speed} = \\dfrac{\\text{Distance}}{\\text{Time}} \\)","\\( = \\dfrac{12 \\text{ km}}{3 \\text{ hrs}} \\)","\\( = 4 \\text{ kph} \\)"],"hint":"Use the formula: \\( \\text{Speed} = \\dfrac{\\text{Distance}}{\\text{Time}} \\).","image":null,"topic":null,"type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"If \\( 7(x - 2) = 3x - 2 \\), what is the value of \\( x \\)?","options":["\\( -4 \\)","\\( -3 \\)","\\( 2 \\)","\\( 3 \\)","\\( 4 \\)"],"correctIndex":3,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\( 7(x - 2) = 3x - 2 \\)","\\( 7x - 14 = 3x - 2 \\)","\\( 7x - 3x = -2 + 14 \\)","\\( 4x = 12 \\)","\\( x = \\dfrac{12}{4} \\)","\\( x = 3 \\)"],"hint":"Expand the bracket on the left side first, then collect like terms to isolate \\( x \\).","image":null,"topic":null,"type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"Study the sample below and answer the given question (Find S):","options":["\\( 1 \\)","\\( 65 \\)","\\( 194 \\)","\\( 169 \\)","\\( 196 \\)"],"correctIndex":1,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\( \\text{Left} = 13, \\quad \\text{Top} = 5 \\)","\\( S = 13 \\times 5 \\)","\\( = 65 \\)"],"hint":"Look for an arithmetic operation linking the values. Try multiplication.","image":"/exam-archive/competitions/scholastic/upper-primary/2025/first-Round/q26.svg","topic":null,"type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"Study the sample carefully and answer the following question (Find ?):","options":["\\( 37 \\)","\\( 30 \\)","\\( 24 \\)","\\( 14 \\)","\\( 5 \\)"],"correctIndex":2,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\( \\text{Following the operational rule of the sequence:} \\)","\\( ? = 24 \\)"],"hint":"Identify the mathematical rule combining the top number and the bottom number in the sequence.","image":"/exam-archive/competitions/scholastic/upper-primary/2025/first-Round/q27.svg","topic":null,"type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"Which of the following is an improper fraction?","options":["\\( \\dfrac{2}{3} \\)","\\( \\dfrac{3}{2} \\)","\\( 1\\dfrac{2}{3} \\)","\\( \\dfrac{1}{3} \\)","\\( \\dfrac{1}{2} \\)"],"correctIndex":1,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\( \\text{Improper fraction: numerator} \\geq \\text{denominator} \\)","\\( \\dfrac{3}{2}: \\quad 3 > 2 \\)","\\( \\therefore \\dfrac{3}{2} \\text{ is an improper fraction} \\)"],"hint":"An improper fraction has a numerator greater than or equal to its denominator.","image":null,"topic":null,"type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"You had \\( \\$10{,}000.00 \\) in your account, you withdrew \\( 10\\% \\) of your current balance. How much money is your new balance?","options":["\\( \\$1{,}000.00 \\)","\\( \\$8{,}000.00 \\)","\\( \\$9{,}000.00 \\)","\\( \\$10{,}000.00 \\)","\\( \\$11{,}000.00 \\)"],"correctIndex":2,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\( \\text{Withdrawal} = 10\\% \\times 10{,}000 \\)","\\( = \\dfrac{10}{100} \\times 10{,}000 \\)","\\( = 1{,}000 \\)","\\( \\text{New balance} = 10{,}000 - 1{,}000 \\)","\\( = \\$9{,}000 \\)"],"hint":"Calculate \\( 10\\% \\) of \\( \\$10{,}000 \\), then subtract from the original balance.","image":null,"topic":null,"type":"objective","section":null,"film":"withdraw-10"},{"question":"A triangle in which two sides are equal is called a _____ triangle?","options":["Equilateral","Isosceles","Right angle","Scalene","Isometric"],"correctIndex":1,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\( \\text{Equilateral: all 3 sides equal} \\)","\\( \\text{Isosceles: exactly 2 sides equal} \\)","\\( \\text{Scalene: all 3 sides different} \\)","\\( \\therefore \\text{2 equal sides} \\Rightarrow \\text{Isosceles triangle} \\)"],"hint":"Recall the classification of triangles based on their side lengths.","image":null,"topic":null,"type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"Of the following fractions, which is less than \\( \\dfrac{2}{3} \\)?","options":["\\( \\dfrac{7}{8} \\)","\\( \\dfrac{5}{6} \\)","\\( \\dfrac{3}{4} \\)","\\( \\dfrac{3}{5} \\)","\\( \\dfrac{5}{7} \\)"],"correctIndex":3,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\( \\dfrac{2}{3} \\approx 0.667 \\)","\\( \\dfrac{7}{8} = 0.875 > 0.667 \\)","\\( \\dfrac{5}{6} \\approx 0.833 > 0.667 \\)","\\( \\dfrac{3}{4} = 0.750 > 0.667 \\)","\\( \\dfrac{3}{5} = 0.600 < 0.667 \\)","\\( \\dfrac{5}{7} \\approx 0.714 > 0.667 \\)","\\( \\therefore \\dfrac{3}{5} \\text{ is the only fraction less than } \\dfrac{2}{3} \\)"],"hint":"Convert each fraction to a decimal and compare with \\( \\dfrac{2}{3} \\approx 0.667 \\).","image":null,"topic":null,"type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"How many factors does the number \\( 12 \\) have?","options":["\\( 3 \\)","\\( 6 \\)","\\( 9 \\)","\\( 12 \\)","\\( 18 \\)"],"correctIndex":1,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\( 12 = 1 \\times 12 \\)","\\( 12 = 2 \\times 6 \\)","\\( 12 = 3 \\times 4 \\)","\\( \\text{Factors} = \\{1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 12\\} \\)","\\( \\text{Count} = 6 \\)"],"hint":"List all whole numbers that divide evenly into \\( 12 \\) without leaving a remainder.","image":null,"topic":null,"type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"The digits \\( 2, 3, 5, 6 \\) and \\( 9 \\) are each used once to form the greatest possible odd five-digit number. The digit in the tens place is?","options":["\\( 5 \\)","\\( 9 \\)","\\( 3 \\)","\\( 6 \\)","\\( 2 \\)"],"correctIndex":4,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\( \\text{Digits: } \\{2, 3, 5, 6, 9\\} \\)","\\( \\text{Units digit must be odd} \\Rightarrow \\text{use smallest odd digit} = 3 \\)","\\( \\text{Remaining: } \\{9, 6, 5, 2\\} \\Rightarrow \\text{arrange descending} \\)","\\( \\text{Number} = 96523 \\)","\\( \\text{Tens digit} = 2 \\)"],"hint":"To maximise the number, arrange digits in descending order. The units digit must be odd — use the smallest odd digit available.","image":null,"topic":null,"type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"Which one is the equivalent fraction of \\( \\dfrac{1}{2} \\)?","options":["\\( \\dfrac{6}{12} \\)","\\( \\dfrac{3}{5} \\)","\\( \\dfrac{1}{4} \\)","\\( \\dfrac{2}{5} \\)","\\( \\dfrac{2}{8} \\)"],"correctIndex":0,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\( \\dfrac{6}{12} = \\dfrac{6 \\div 6}{12 \\div 6} \\)","\\( = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\)","\\( \\therefore \\dfrac{6}{12} \\equiv \\dfrac{1}{2} \\)"],"hint":"Simplify each fraction to see which one reduces to \\( \\dfrac{1}{2} \\).","image":null,"topic":null,"type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"Write \\( 48 \\) in index form","options":["\\( 2^4 \\times 3 \\)","\\( 2^3 \\times 3 \\)","\\( 2^5 \\times 3 \\)","\\( 2^3 \\times 3^2 \\)","\\( 2 \\times 24 \\)"],"correctIndex":0,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\( 48 \\div 2 = 24 \\)","\\( 24 \\div 2 = 12 \\)","\\( 12 \\div 2 = 6 \\)","\\( 6 \\div 2 = 3 \\)","\\( 48 = 2 \\times 2 \\times 2 \\times 2 \\times 3 \\)","\\( = 2^4 \\times 3 \\)"],"hint":"Find the prime factorization of \\( 48 \\) by repeatedly dividing by prime numbers.","image":null,"topic":null,"type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"The smallest decimal among the following is?","options":["\\( 0.0634 \\)","\\( 0.634 \\)","\\( 0.0643 \\)","\\( 1.634 \\)","\\( 0.6001 \\)"],"correctIndex":0,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\( 1.634 \\text{ — largest (integer part = 1)} \\)","\\( 0.634, 0.6001 \\text{ — tenths digit = 6} \\)","\\( 0.0634, 0.0643 \\text{ — tenths digit = 0 (smaller)} \\)","\\( \\text{Compare thousandths: } 0.0634 \\text{ has } 3, \\quad 0.0643 \\text{ has } 4 \\)","\\( 3 < 4 \\)","\\( \\therefore 0.0634 \\text{ is the smallest} \\)"],"hint":"Compare digits from left to right: integer part first, then tenths, hundredths, thousandths.","image":null,"topic":null,"type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"The perimeter of a rectangle is \\( 60 \\text{ cm} \\). Its breadth is \\( 10 \\text{ cm} \\), find the length.","options":["\\( 10 \\text{ cm} \\)","\\( 20 \\text{ cm} \\)","\\( 30 \\text{ cm} \\)","\\( 40 \\text{ cm} \\)","\\( 50 \\text{ cm} \\)"],"correctIndex":1,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\( P = 2(l + b) \\)","\\( 60 = 2(l + 10) \\)","\\( \\dfrac{60}{2} = l + 10 \\)","\\( 30 = l + 10 \\)","\\( l = 30 - 10 \\)","\\( l = 20 \\text{ cm} \\)"],"hint":"Use the formula: \\( P = 2(l + b) \\). Substitute \\( P = 60 \\) and \\( b = 10 \\), then solve for \\( l \\).","image":null,"topic":null,"type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"Evaluate \\( 4 - 0.6 \\)","options":["\\( 0.2 \\)","\\( 2.0 \\)","\\( 2.2 \\)","\\( 3.4 \\)","\\( 4.6 \\)"],"correctIndex":3,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\( 4.0 - 0.6 \\)","\\( = 3.4 \\)"],"hint":"Write \\( 4 \\) as \\( 4.0 \\) to align the decimal points, then subtract.","image":null,"topic":null,"type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"Divide \\( 3.888 \\) by \\( 7.2 \\)","options":["\\( 5.4 \\)","\\( 0.54 \\)","\\( 0.054 \\)","\\( 0.0054 \\)","\\( 54 \\)"],"correctIndex":1,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\( \\dfrac{3.888}{7.2} = \\dfrac{3.888 \\times 10}{7.2 \\times 10} \\)","\\( = \\dfrac{38.88}{72} \\)","\\( = 0.54 \\)"],"hint":"Multiply both numbers by \\( 10 \\) to remove the decimal from the divisor, then divide \\( 38.88 \\) by \\( 72 \\).","image":null,"topic":null,"type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"Which of the following is both a perfect cube and perfect square?","options":["\\( 64 \\)","\\( 81 \\)","\\( 9 \\)","\\( 40 \\)","\\( 110 \\)"],"correctIndex":0,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\( 64 = 8^2 \\Rightarrow \\text{perfect square} \\)","\\( 64 = 4^3 \\Rightarrow \\text{perfect cube} \\)","\\( 64 = 2^6 \\)","\\( 81 = 9^2 \\text{ but } 81 \\neq n^3 \\text{ for integer } n \\)","\\( 9 = 3^2 \\text{ but } 9 \\neq n^3 \\text{ for integer } n \\)","\\( \\therefore 64 \\text{ is both a perfect square and perfect cube} \\)"],"hint":"A number that is both a perfect square and perfect cube satisfies \\( n = x^6 \\) for some integer \\( x \\).","image":null,"topic":null,"type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"What is the value of \\( n \\) in \\( 1{,}420{,}628 + n = 3{,}928{,}716 \\)?","options":["\\( 2{,}508{,}088 \\)","\\( 2{,}508{,}188 \\)","\\( 5{,}349{,}344 \\)","\\( 2{,}408{,}088 \\)","\\( 2{,}518{,}088 \\)"],"correctIndex":0,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\( 1{,}420{,}628 + n = 3{,}928{,}716 \\)","\\( n = 3{,}928{,}716 - 1{,}420{,}628 \\)","\\( n = 2{,}508{,}088 \\)"],"hint":"Subtract \\( 1{,}420{,}628 \\) from both sides of the equation to isolate \\( n \\).","image":null,"topic":null,"type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"Divide \\( 2.58 \\) by \\( 0.7 \\), giving the answer correct to \\( 2 \\) decimal places.","options":["\\( 3.68 \\)","\\( 3.69 \\)","\\( 3.70 \\)","\\( 0.37 \\)","\\( 36.86 \\)"],"correctIndex":1,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\( \\dfrac{2.58}{0.7} = \\dfrac{25.8}{7} \\)","\\( 25.8 \\div 7 = 3.685\\overline{7}\\ldots \\)","\\( \\text{3rd decimal digit} = 5 \\geq 5 \\Rightarrow \\text{round up} \\)","\\( \\approx 3.69 \\)"],"hint":"Multiply both numbers by \\( 10 \\) to get \\( 25.8 \\div 7 \\). Calculate to 3 d.p., then round.","image":null,"topic":null,"type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"Simplify \\( 7 \\times 6 + 8 \\div 2 \\)","options":["\\( 25 \\)","\\( 46 \\)","\\( 38 \\)","\\( 45 \\)","\\( 50 \\)"],"correctIndex":1,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\( 7 \\times 6 = 42 \\)","\\( 8 \\div 2 = 4 \\)","\\( 42 + 4 = 46 \\)"],"hint":"Follow BODMAS: perform division and multiplication before addition.","image":null,"topic":null,"type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"Express \\( \\dfrac{1}{8} \\) as a percentage","options":["\\( 12.5\\% \\)","\\( 8\\% \\)","\\( 0.125\\% \\)","\\( 80\\% \\)","\\( 1.25\\% \\)"],"correctIndex":0,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\( \\dfrac{1}{8} \\times 100\\% \\)","\\( = \\dfrac{100}{8}\\% \\)","\\( = 12.5\\% \\)"],"hint":"Multiply the fraction by \\( 100\\% \\) to convert it to a percentage.","image":null,"topic":null,"type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"Taiwo, Kehinde and Idowu are to share \\( \\$600 \\) in the ratio \\( 3:2:1 \\) respectively. What is Idowu's share?","options":["\\( \\$100 \\)","\\( \\$200 \\)","\\( \\$300 \\)","\\( \\$400 \\)","\\( \\$50 \\)"],"correctIndex":0,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\( \\text{Total parts} = 3 + 2 + 1 = 6 \\)","\\( \\text{Value of 1 part} = \\dfrac{\\$600}{6} \\)","\\( = \\$100 \\)","\\( \\text{Idowu's share} = 1 \\times \\$100 \\)","\\( = \\$100 \\)"],"hint":"Find the total number of ratio parts, then determine the value of one part.","image":null,"topic":null,"type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"Ayodeji is thinking of two whole numbers. Their product is \\( 132 \\) and their sum is \\( 23 \\). What is the smaller number?","options":["\\( 11 \\)","\\( 12 \\)","\\( 6 \\)","\\( 22 \\)","\\( 2 \\)"],"correctIndex":0,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\( x \\times y = 132, \\quad x + y = 23 \\)","\\( 1 \\times 132 \\Rightarrow \\text{sum} = 133 \\)","\\( 2 \\times 66 \\Rightarrow \\text{sum} = 68 \\)","\\( 3 \\times 44 \\Rightarrow \\text{sum} = 47 \\)","\\( 4 \\times 33 \\Rightarrow \\text{sum} = 37 \\)","\\( 6 \\times 22 \\Rightarrow \\text{sum} = 28 \\)","\\( 11 \\times 12 \\Rightarrow \\text{sum} = 23 \\checkmark \\)","\\( \\therefore \\text{smaller number} = 11 \\)"],"hint":"Find factor pairs of \\( 132 \\) that add up to \\( 23 \\).","image":null,"topic":null,"type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"Find the value of \\( 6\\dfrac{1}{4}\\% \\) of \\( \\$1200 \\)","options":["\\( \\$60 \\)","\\( \\$72 \\)","\\( \\$75 \\)","\\( \\$80 \\)","\\( \\$125 \\)"],"correctIndex":2,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\( 6\\dfrac{1}{4}\\% = \\dfrac{25}{4}\\% \\)","\\( = \\dfrac{25}{400} \\)","\\( = \\dfrac{1}{16} \\)","\\( \\text{Value} = \\dfrac{1}{16} \\times 1200 \\)","\\( = \\dfrac{1200}{16} \\)","\\( = \\$75 \\)"],"hint":"Convert \\( 6\\dfrac{1}{4}\\% \\) to a fraction: \\( \\dfrac{25}{4}\\% = \\dfrac{25}{400} = \\dfrac{1}{16} \\), then multiply by \\( 1200 \\).","image":null,"topic":null,"type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"If \\( £120 \\) is shared between Cole, Ella, and Vicky. Cole receives \\( \\dfrac{5}{8} \\) of the money and Ella receives \\( \\dfrac{2}{3} \\) of the remainder while Vicky received the rest. How much more money does Cole receive than Vicky?","options":["\\( £45 \\)","\\( £50 \\)","\\( £60 \\)","\\( £75 \\)","\\( £15 \\)"],"correctIndex":2,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\( \\text{Cole} = \\dfrac{5}{8} \\times 120 \\)","\\( = £75 \\)","\\( \\text{Remainder} = 120 - 75 \\)","\\( = £45 \\)","\\( \\text{Ella} = \\dfrac{2}{3} \\times 45 \\)","\\( = £30 \\)","\\( \\text{Vicky} = 45 - 30 \\)","\\( = £15 \\)","\\( \\text{Cole} - \\text{Vicky} = 75 - 15 \\)","\\( = £60 \\)"],"hint":"Calculate Cole's share first, then find the remainder, then Ella's and Vicky's shares.","image":null,"topic":null,"type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"The diagram below shows \\( 3 \\) angles on a straight line. What is the sum of the two smaller angles?","options":["\\( 105^{\\circ} \\)","\\( 75^{\\circ} \\)","\\( 120^{\\circ} \\)","\\( 45^{\\circ} \\)","\\( 135^{\\circ} \\)"],"correctIndex":0,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\( 60 + 5x + 3x = 180 \\)","\\( 60 + 8x = 180 \\)","\\( 8x = 120 \\)","\\( x = \\dfrac{120}{8} \\)","\\( x = 15 \\)","\\( 5x = 5 \\times 15 = 75^{\\circ} \\)","\\( 3x = 3 \\times 15 = 45^{\\circ} \\)","\\( \\text{Two smaller angles} = 45^{\\circ} + 60^{\\circ} \\)","\\( = 105^{\\circ} \\)"],"hint":"Angles on a straight line add up to \\( 180^{\\circ} \\). The three angles are \\( 60^{\\circ} \\), \\( 5x^{\\circ} \\), and \\( 3x^{\\circ} \\).","image":"/exam-archive/competitions/scholastic/upper-primary/2025/first-Round/q49.svg","topic":null,"type":"objective","section":null},{"question":"What is the area of the square below in \\( \\text{cm}^2 \\)?","options":["\\( 10 \\)","\\( 20 \\)","\\( 25 \\)","\\( 5 \\)","\\( 15 \\)"],"correctIndex":2,"answer":null,"accept":null,"explanation":["\\( s = 5 \\text{ cm} \\)","\\( A = s^2 \\)","\\( = 5^2 \\)","\\( = 25 \\text{ cm}^2 \\)"],"hint":"The area of a square is \\( s^2 \\), where \\( s \\) is the side length.","image":"/exam-archive/competitions/scholastic/upper-primary/2025/first-Round/q50.svg","topic":null,"type":"objective","section":null}]}